{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And YHWH uncovered the eyes of Balaam</b> – From this verse we learn that Balaam was not a prophet. If he had been a prophet, how would he need the uncovering of the eyes to see the angel, as Scripture says concerning one who had not attained prophecy, such as the lad of Elisha (II Kings 6:17) and Hagar the Egyptian (Genesis 21:19), and it does not say thus of the prophets. And thus Scripture calls him \"Balaam son of Beor the diviner\" (Joshua 13:22). And what he said, \"as YHWH speaks to me\" (Numbers 22:8) — he calls his knowledge of future events through his divinations the word of YHWH. But for the honor of Israel HaShem came to him that night, and after that he merited the uncovering of the eyes to see the angel, and He spoke with him, and at the end he ascended to the level of the vision of Shaddai (below 24:4, 16), all for the sake of Israel and their honor. And after he returned to his land he was a diviner, for thus Scripture calls him at his death: \"And Balaam son of Beor the diviner they slew with the sword\" (Joshua 13:22) — and far be it that they would lay a hand upon a prophet of HaShem. And thus they said in Midrash Bamidbar Sinai Rabbah (Numbers Rabbah 20:19): Balaam was visited by the holy spirit, and when he encountered Balak the holy spirit departed from him and he returned to being a diviner as at first; therefore he cried out: I was exalted, and Balak brought me down."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:49.415767886Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}